GFX(3)                         P32W Unix                          GFX(3)

NAME
       gfx, gopen, gline, gcircle, gtext - draw on the video screen

SYNOPSIS
       #include <gfx.h>

       int  gopen(void);
       void gclose(void);
       int  gflush(void);
       int  gsync(void);

       void gclear(void);
       void gink(int ink);
       void gclip(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1);

       void gpoint(int x, int y);
       int  gget(int x, int y);
       void gline(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1);
       void gmove(int x, int y);
       void gdraw(int x, int y);
       void gpoly(const int *xy, int n);
       void gbox(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1);
       void gfbox(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1);
       void gcircle(int x, int y, int r);
       void gfcircle(int x, int y, int r);
       void gellipse(int x, int y, int rx, int ry);
       void gfellipse(int x, int y, int rx, int ry);
       void garc(int x, int y, int rx, int ry, int a0, int a1);

       void gtext(int x, int y, const char *s);
       void glook(int look);

       int  gsin(int degrees);
       int  gcos(int degrees);

       void gwords(const unsigned short *w, int n);
       int  gfd(void);

       extern int gwidth, gheight, gcellw, gcellh;

       cc prog.c -lgfx

DESCRIPTION
       The library draws on the video card's screen: 640 by 480
       dots, each lit or not. It works through /dev/video
       (video(4)): the card's chip does the drawing, and the
       library sends it its commands.

       gopen takes the screen and clears it. From then until
       gclose, or the end of the program, the console's text is
       not shown; afterwards it is back as if nothing had been
       drawn. One program at a time may have the screen, and only
       root and group staff.

       The library gathers what it has to send and writes it a
       buffer at a time. Nothing need show until gflush, which
       sends what has been drawn so far. gsync does the same and
       then waits until the chip has drawn it.

THE SCREEN
       x 0, y 0 is the bottom-left dot. x runs to the right, up
       to gwidth - 1 (639); y runs up, to gheight - 1 (479). A
       figure may lie partly or wholly off the screen: only what
       is on it is drawn. gclip makes that true of a smaller
       rectangle (two opposite corners, both inside it) until it
       is called again;
              gclip(0, 0, gwidth - 1, gheight - 1)
       is the whole screen again.

       gclear puts every dot out, whatever the clipping. gget
       gives 1 if a dot is lit and 0 if it is not, or is off the
       screen.

INK
       gink chooses what drawing does to a dot:

       G_WHITE   lights it (as after gopen)
       G_BLACK   puts it out
       G_XOR     turns it over: lit if it was out, out if lit

       Every routine draws each dot of its figure once. So a
       figure drawn a second time in G_XOR is gone, and what was
       under it is as it was: the way to move a thing over a
       picture (EXAMPLES). Two figures that cross, and two sides
       of an outline where they run into a sharp corner, share
       dots as any two figures do.

FIGURES
       gpoint is one dot. gline is a line with both its ends.

       gmove puts the pen somewhere, drawing nothing. gdraw draws
       a line from the pen to x, y, where the pen then is. Lines
       drawn one after another this way are joined: the dot where
       two of them meet is drawn once, and so is the first dot of
       a chain that comes back to where it began. Any other
       routine, gflush too, ends the chain.

       gpoly is the closed outline through n points: xy has the x
       and then the y of each.

       gbox is a rectangle's outline, given two opposite corners;
       gfbox is the rectangle filled.

       gcircle is a circle about x, y; gfcircle is it filled.
       gellipse and gfellipse are an ellipse rx to each side of
       its centre and ry above and below it. The dots are square,
       so a circle is round. A radius may be up to 16383.

       garc is the part of that ellipse from the direction a0
       round to a1: degrees as seen from the centre, 0 to the
       right, 90 up, anticlockwise. With a1 less than a0 the arc
       still runs anticlockwise, through 0; with a1 a whole turn
       or more past a0 it is all of the ellipse.

TEXT
       gtext draws s in the console's font, each character gcellw
       (8) dots wide and gcellh (16) high; x, y is the bottom-left
       dot of the first one's cell. A newline in s starts again
       at x, one cell lower. A character the font has not (the
       printable ASCII ones are all it has) is drawn as ?.

       The ink says what happens to the dots of the letters: lit,
       put out, or turned over. The rest of each cell is left as
       it was. glook changes the look, from then on; add together

       G_BOLD     bold
       G_UNDER    underlined
       G_OPAQUE   the rest of each cell is drawn too, in the other
                  colour: white letters on black, or with
                  G_BLACK black letters on white. Text written
                  this way covers what was there, other text
                  too.

       glook(0) is plain again.

SINES
       gsin and gcos give the sine and cosine of a whole number
       of degrees, any number, multiplied by G_ONE (16384): for
       turning things without floating point. The point d from
       x, y in the direction a is
              x + d * gcos(a) / G_ONE,  y + d * gsin(a) / G_ONE

THE CHIP ITSELF
       gwords sends n of the chip's own command words (video(4)),
       in their turn among what the library sends. gfd gives the
       file descriptor of /dev/video, to read what such a command
       reads: call gflush first. The library depends on the
       chip's origin, pattern and mask being as it finds them,
       and on colour register 1 and the area registers 8 to 0x0B
       being as it left them: a program that changes one of
       these puts it back.

RETURN VALUE
       gopen returns 0, or -1 with errno set as open(2) sets it:
       ENXIO no video card, EBUSY another program has the screen,
       EACCES not allowed. gflush and gsync return 0, or -1 if
       something could not be written since the last time (or
       the screen is not open).

EXAMPLES
       /usr/examples/clock.c
              a clock: its face drawn once, its hands in G_XOR

       #include <gfx.h>
       #include <unistd.h>

       int main(void)
       {
           if (gopen() < 0) return 1;
           gbox(0, 0, gwidth - 1, gheight - 1);
           gcircle(320, 240, 100);
           gtext(304, 232, "P32W");
           gflush();
           sleep(5);
           gclose();
           return 0;
       }

       cc -o ring ring.c -lgfx

FILES
       /usr/lib/libgfx.a, /usr/include/gfx.h, /dev/video
       /usr/src/libgfx    its source

SEE ALSO
       video(4), cc(1)

LIMITATIONS
       Black and white only. There are no filled outlines other
       than boxes, circles and ellipses, no patterns or dashed
       lines, and no way to read the mouse.

       The library works out a circle's dots itself and has the
       chip draw them as short runs, so that they are the same
       on any chip and each is drawn once; the chip's own circle
       command would be faster. Which dots a slanted line has is
       the chip's choice.

       An ellipse far larger than the screen (not a circle) is
       drawn to fewer places, and may be a dot out here and
       there.
